  "title": "Computing an area exactly.",
  "body": "Computing an area exactly  Recall that we wish to compute the area of and that our strategy is to approximate this area by the area of a union of a large number of very thin rectangles, and then take the limit as the number of rectangles goes to infinity. In Example , we used just four rectangles. Now we'll consider a general number of rectangles, that we'll call . Then we'll take the limit . So  pick a natural number and  subdivide the interval into equal subintervals each of width , and  subdivide the area of interest into corresponding thin strips, as in the figure below.  The area we want is exactly the sum of the areas of all of the thin strips.     Each of these strips is almost, but not quite, a rectangle. As in Example , the only problem is that the top is not horizontal. So we approximate each strip by a rectangle, just by levelling off the top. Again, we have to make a choice at what height do we level off the top?  Consider, for example, the leftmost strip. On this strip, runs from to . As runs from to , the height runs from to . It would be reasonable to choose the height of the approximating rectangle to be somewhere between and . Which height should we choose?  Well, as we said in Example , it doesn't matter. We shall shortly take the limit and, in that limit, all of those different choices give exactly the same final answer. We won't justify that statement in this example, but there will be an (optional) section shortly that provides the justification. For this example we just, arbitrarily, choose the height of each rectangle to be the height of the graph at the smallest value of in the corresponding strip Notice that since is an increasing function, this choice of heights means that each of our rectangles is smaller than the strip it came from. . The figure on the left below shows the approximating rectangles when and the figure on the right shows the approximating rectangles when .      Now we compute the approximating area when there are strips.  We approximate the leftmost strip by a rectangle of height . All of the rectangles have width . So the leftmost rectangle has area .  On strip number , runs from to . So the smallest value of on strip number is , and we approximate strip number by a rectangle of height and hence of area .  And so on.  On the last strip, runs from to . So the smallest value of on the last strip is , and we approximate the last strip by a rectangle of height and hence of area .    The total area of all of the approximating rectangles is Now the sum in the brackets might look a little intimidating because of all the exponentials, but it actually has a pretty simple structure that can be easily seen if we rename . Then  the first term is 1 = and  the second term is and  the third term is and  the fourth term is and  and so on and  the last term is .  So The sum in brackets is known as a geometric sum and satisfies a nice simple formula:  Geometric sum      The derivation of the above formula is not too difficult. So let's derive it in a little aside.  Geometric sum  Denote the sum as Notice that if we multiply the whole sum by we get back almost the same thing: This right hand side differs from the original sum only in that  the right hand side, which starts with , is missing the that starts with, and  the right hand side has an extra at the end that does not appear in .  That is as required. Notice that the last step in the manipulations only works providing (otherwise we are dividing by zero).   Now we can go back to our area approximation armed with the above result about geometric sums.   To get the exact area We haven't proved that this will give us the exact area, but it should be clear that taking this limit will give us a lower bound on the area. To complete things rigorously we also need an upper bound and the squeeze theorem. We do this in the next optional subsection. all we need to do is make the approximation better and better by taking the limit . The limit will look more familiar if we rename to . As tends to infinity, tends to , so Examining this limit we see that both numerator and denominator tend to zero as , and so we cannot evaluate this limit by computing the limits of the numerator and denominator separately and then dividing the results. Despite this, the limit is not too hard to evaluate; here we give two ways:  Perhaps the easiest way to compute the limit is by using l'Hôpital's rule If you do not recall L'Hôpital's rule and indeterminate forms then we recommend you skim over your differential calculus notes on the topic. . Since both numerator and denominator go to zero, this is a indeterminate form. Thus   Another way Say if you don't recall l'Hôpital's rule and have not had time to revise it. to evaluate the same limit is to observe that it can be massaged into the form of the limit definition of the derivative. First notice that provided this second limit exists and is nonzero To hyphenate or not to hypenate: non-zero or nonzero ? The authors took our lead from here and also here . . This second limit should look a little familiar: which is just the definition of the derivative of at . Hence we have   So, after this short aside into limits, we may now conclude that   "
