  "body": " We want to compute the area of an ellipse, for some (let's say positive) constants and .  Solve the equation for the upper half of the ellipse. It should have the form   Write an integral for the area of the upper half of the ellipse. Using properties of integrals, make the integrand look like the upper half of a circle.  Using geometry and your answer to part (b), find the area of the ellipse.    In part (b), you'll have to factor a constant out through a square root. Remember the upper half of a circle looks like .  (a)   (b)   (c)        The values of in the domain of the function above are those that satisfy . That is, . Therefore, the upper half of the ellipse has area    The function is the upper-half of the circle centred at the origin with radius . So, the expression from (b) evaluates to .  The expression from (b) was half of the ellipse, so the area of the ellipse is .     Remark: this was a slightly long-winded way of getting the result. The reasoning is basically this:  The area of the unit circle is .  The ellipse is obtained by shrinking the unit circle horizontally by a factor of . So, its area is .  Further, the ellipse is obtained from the previous ellipse by shrinking it vertically by a factor of . So, its area is .    "
