  "title": "",
  "body": "  Fill in the following table: the product of an (even\/odd) function with an (even\/odd) function is an (even\/odd) function. You may assume that both functions are defined for all real numbers.      even odd   even   odd      For two functions and , define . If , then the product is even; if , then the product is odd.  The table will not be the same as if we were multiplying even and odd numbers .      even odd   even even odd   odd odd even      Let's recall the definitions of even and odd functions: is even if for every in its domain, and is odd if for every in its domain.  Let .  even even: If and are both even, then , so their product is even.  odd odd: If and are both odd, then , so their product is even.  even odd: If is even and is odd, then , so their product is odd. Because multiplication is commutative, the order we multiply the functions in doesn't matter.    We note that the table would be the same as if we were adding (not multiplying) even and odd numbers (not functions).  "
