  "body": "Cone  Find the volume of the circular cone of height and radius .   Solution: Here is a sketch of the cone.     We have called the vertical axis , just so that we end up with a integral.     In what follows we will slice the cone into thin horizontal pancakes . In order to approximate the volume of those slices, we need to know the radius of the cone at a height above its point. Consider the cross sections shown in the following figure.     At full height , the cone has radius . If we cut the cone at height , then by similar triangles (see the figure on the right) the radius will be .    Now think of cutting the cone into thin horizontal pancakes . Each such pancake is approximately a squat cylinder of height . This is very similar to how we approximated the area under a curve by tall thin rectangles. Just as we approximated the area under the curve by summing these rectangles, we can approximate the volume of the cone by summing the volumes of these cylinders. Here is a side view of the cone and one of the cylinders.       We follow the method we used in Example , except that our slices are now pancakes instead of rectangles.  Pick a natural number (that we will later send to infinity), then  subdivide the cone into thin pancakes, each of width .  For each , pancake number runs from to , and we approximate its volume by the volume of a squat cone. We pick a number between and and approximate the pancake by a cylinder of height and radius .  Thus the volume of pancake is approximately (as shown in the figure above).     So the Riemann sum approximation of the volume is   By taking the limit as (i.e. taking the limit as the thickness of the pancakes goes to zero), we convert the Riemann sum into a definite integral (see Definition ) and at the same time our approximation of the volume becomes the exact volume:   Our life At least the bits of it involving integrals. would be easier if we could avoid all this formal work with Riemann sums every time we encounter a new volume. So before we compute the above integral, let us redo the above calculation in a less formal manner.   Start again from the picture of the cone     and think of slicing it into thin pancakes, each of width .       The pancake at height above the point of the cone (which is the fraction of the total height of the cone) has  radius (the fraction of the full radius, ) and so  cross-sectional area ,  thickness  we have done something a little sneaky here, see the discussion below.  volume   As runs from to , the total volume is      In this second computation we are using a time-saving trick. As we saw in the formal computation above, what we really need to do is pick a natural number , slice the cone into pancakes each of thickness and then take the limit as . This led to the Riemann sum So knowing that we will replace when we take the limit, we have just skipped the intermediate steps. While this is not entirely rigorous, it can be made so, and does save us a lot of algebra.  "
