  "body": "and  This time we're going to do the two integrals at more or less the same time.    First We have not found but we have related it to .   Now start over with . Once again, we have not found but we have related it back to .   So summarising, we have   So now, substitute the expression for from the second equation into the first equation to get If we substitute the other way around we get That is,   This also says, for example, that is an antiderivative of so that   Note that we can always check whether or not this is correct. It is correct if and only if the derivative of the right hand side is . Here goes. By the product rule which is the desired derivative.  There is another way to find and that, in contrast to the above computations, doesn't involve any trickery. But it does require the use of complex numbers and so is beyond the scope of this course. The secret is to use that and , where is the square root of of the complex number system. See Example .    "
