  "body": " In this example, we integrate .  Solution:     Step 1. Again, we start by comparing the degrees of the numerator and denominator. In this example, the numerator, , has degree four and the denominator, , has degree three. As , we must execute the first step, which is to write in the form with being a polynomial and being a polynomial of degree strictly smaller than the degree of . This step is accomplished by long division, just as we did in Example . We'll go through the whole process in detail again.  Actually before you read on ahead, please have a go at the long division. It is good practice.     We start by observing that to get from the highest degree term in the denominator ( ) to the highest degree term in the numerator ( ), we have to multiply by . So we write,       Now we subtract times the denominator , which is , from the numerator.       The remainder was . To get from the highest degree term in the denominator ( ) to the highest degree term in the remainder ( ), we have to multiply by . So we write,       Now we subtract times the denominator , which is , from the remainder.      This leaves a remainder of . Because the remainder has degree , which is smaller than the degree of the denominator, which is , we stop.  In this example, when we subtracted and from we ended up with . That is, or, collecting the two terms proportional to we get Moving the to the right hand side and dividing the whole equation by gives     This is of the form with the degree of strictly smaller than the degree of , which is what we wanted. Observe, once again, that is the final remainder of the long division procedure and is at the top of the long division computation.        Step 2. The second step is to factor the denominator . In the real world factorisation of polynomials is often very hard. Fortunately One does not typically think of mathematics assignments or exams as nice kind places The polynomials that appear in the real world are not so forgiving. Nature, red in tooth and claw to quote Tennyson inappropriately (especially when this author doesn't know any other words from the poem). , this is not the real world and there is a trick available to help us find this factorisation. The reader should take some time to look at Appendix before proceeding.  The trick exploits the fact that most polynomials that appear in homework assignments and on tests have integer coefficients and some integer roots. Any integer root of a polynomial that has integer coefficients, like , must divide the constant term of the polynomial exactly. Why this is true is explained Appendix contains several simple tricks for factoring polynomials. We recommend that you have a look at them. in Appendix .  So any integer root of must divide exactly. Thus the only integers which can be roots of are and . Of course, not all of these give roots of the polynomial in fact there is no guarantee that any of them will be. We have to test each one.  To test if is a root, we sub into : As , is not a root of .  To test if is a root, we sub it into : As , is a root of . As is a root of , must factor exactly. We can factor the out of by long division once again.   Dividing by gives:     This time, when we subtracted and and from we ended up with  as we knew would happen, because we knew that divides exactly. Hence or or    It isn't quite time to stop yet; we should attempt to factor the quadratic factor, . We can use the quadratic formula To be precise, the quadratic equation has solutions The term is called the discriminant and it tells us about the number of solutions. If the discriminant is positive then there are two real solutions. When it is zero, there is a single solution. And if it is negative, there is no real solutions (you need complex numbers to say more than this). to find the roots of : Since this expression contains the square root of a negative number the equation has no real solutions; without the use of complex numbers, cannot be factored.    We have reached the end of step 2. At this point we have      Step 3. The third step is to write in the form for some constants , and .  Note that the numerator, of the second term on the right hand side is not just a constant. It is of degree one, which is exactly one smaller than the degree of the denominator, . More generally, if the denominator consists of different linear factors and different quadratic factors, then we decompose the ratio as   To determine the values of the constants , we put the right hand side back over the common denominator . The fraction on the far left is the same as the fraction on the far right if and only if their numerators are the same. Again, as in Example , there are a couple of different ways to determine the values of , and from this equation.     Step 3 Algebra Method. The conceptually clearest procedure is to write the right hand side as a polynomial in standard form (i.e. collect up all terms, all terms and all constant terms) For these two polynomials to be the same, the coefficient of on the left hand side and the coefficient of on the right hand side must be the same. Similarly the coefficients of must match and the coefficients of must match.  This gives us a system of three equations in the three unknowns . We can solve this system by  using the first equation, namely , to determine in terms of : .  Substituting into the remaining two equations eliminates the 's from these two equations, leaving two equations in the two unknowns and .   Now we can use the equation , to determine in terms of : .  Substituting this into the remaining equation eliminates the 's leaving an equation in the one unknown , which is easy to solve.   So , and then , and then . Hence        Step 3 Sneaky Method. While the above method is transparent, it is rather tedious. It is arguably better to use the second, sneakier and more efficient, procedure. In order for the equation must hold for all values of .  In particular, it must be true for . When , the factor multiplying is exactly zero. So and disappear from the equation, leaving us with an easy equation to solve for :   Sub this value of back in and simplify. Since is a factor on the right hand side, it must also be a factor on the left hand side.   So again we find that   Thus our integrand can be written as      Step 4. Now we can finally integrate! The first two pieces are easy. (We're leaving the arbitrary constant to the end of the computation.)  The final piece is a little harder. The idea is to complete the square This same idea arose in Section . Given a quadratic written as rewrite it as . We can determine and by expanding and comparing coefficients of : . Hence and . in the denominator and then make a change of variables to make the fraction look like . In this case so we make the change of variables  Both integrals are easily evaluated, using the substitution , for the first. That's finally it. Putting all of the pieces together      "
