  "body": "1997D  The solid is 40 cm high and the horizontal cross sections are circular disks. The table below gives the diameters of the cross sections in centimeters at 10 cm intervals. Use the trapezoidal rule to estimate the volume of .     height 0 10 20 30 40   diameter 24 16 10 6 4      See Section . To set up the volume integral, see Example . Note the dimensions given for the cross sections are diameters, not radii.     Let denote the diameter at height . As in Example , we slice into thin horizontal pancakes , which in this case are circular.      We are told that the pancake at height is a circular disk of diameter and so  has cross-sectional area and thickness and hence  has volume .    Hence the volume of is where we have approximated the integral using the trapezoidal rule with , and used a calculator to get a decimal approximation.   "
