  "body": "2000D  Define a function and an integral by Estimate how many subdivisions are needed to calculate to five decimal places of accuracy using the trapezoidal rule.  Note that if is the error using subintervals, then , where is the maximum absolute value of the second derivative of the function being integrated and and are the limits of integration.   See Example .  You'll have to differentiate . To that end, you may also want to review the fundamental theorem of calculus and, in particular, Example .  You don't have to find the best possible value for . A reasonable upper bound on will do.  To have five decimal places of accuracy, your error must be less than 0.000005. This ensures that, if you round your approximation to five decimal places, they will all be correct.     Let . By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 1, . By its definition, , so we use the chain rule to differentiate . Since , we have and, for , . When the trapezoidal rule with subintervals is applied, the resulting error obeys Any integer will do.  "
