  "title": "Hooke’s Law.",
  "body": "Hooke's Law  Imagine that a spring lies along the -axis. The left hand end is fixed to a wall, but the right hand end lies freely at . So the spring is at its natural length .       Now suppose that we wish to stretch out the spring so that its right hand end is at .  Hooke's Law Robert Hooke (1635 1703) was an English contemporary of Isaac Newton (1643 1727). It was in a 1676 letter to Hooke that Newton wrote If I have seen further it is by standing on the shoulders of Giants. There is some thought that this was sarcasm and Newton was actually making fun of Hooke, who had a spinal deformity. However at that time Hooke and Newton were still friends. Several years later they did have a somewhat public falling-out over some of Newton's work on optics. says that when a (linear) spring is stretched (or compressed) by units beyond its natural length, it exerts a force of magnitude , where the constant is the spring constant of that spring.  In our case, once we have stretched the spring by units to the right, the spring will be trying to pull back the right hand end by applying a force of magnitude directed to the left.  For us to continue stretching the spring we will have to apply a compensating force of magnitude directed to the right. That is, we have to apply the force .  So to stretch a spring by units from its natural length we have to supply the work     "
