  "title": "Escape Velocity.",
  "body": "Escape Velocity  Suppose that you shoot a probe straight up from the surface of the Earth at what initial speed must the probe move in order to escape Earth's gravity?   Solution: We determine this by computing how much work must be done in order to escape Earth's gravity. If we assume that all of this work comes from the probe's initial kinetic energy, then we can establish the minimum initial velocity required.    The work done by gravity when a mass moves from the surface of the Earth to a height above the surface is where is the gravitational force acting on the mass at height above the Earth's surface.  The gravitational force Newton published his inverse square law of universal gravitation in his Principia in 1687. His law states that the gravitational force between two masses and is where is the distance separating the (centres of the) masses and is the gravitational constant. Notice that measures the separation between the centres of the masses not the distance between the surfaces of the objects. Also, do not confuse with  standard acceleration due to gravity. The first measurement of was performed by Henry Cavendish in 1798 the interested reader should look up the Cavendish experiment for details of this very impressive work. of the Earth acting on a particle of mass at a height above the surface of the Earth is where is the gravitational constant, is the mass of the Earth and is the radius of the Earth. Note that is the distance from the object to the centre of the Earth. Additionally, note that this force is negative because gravity acts downward.  So the work done by gravity on the probe, as it travels from the surface of the Earth to a height , is   So if the probe completely escapes the Earth and travels all the way to , gravity does work The minus sign means that gravity has removed energy from the probe.  To finish the problem we need one more assumption. Let us assume that all of this energy comes from the probe's initial kinetic energy and that the probe is not fitted with any sort of rocket engine. Hence the initial kinetic energy (coming from an initial velocity ) must be at least as large as the work computed above. That is we need   The right hand side of this inequality, , is called the escape velocity.    "
