  "url": "sec_work.html#sec_work-4-1-4",
  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "2.1.2.2",
  "title": "",
  "body": " A rock exerts a force of 1 N on the ground where it sits due to gravity. Use m\/sec .  What is the mass of the rock?  How much work (in joules) does it take to lift that rock one metre in the air?  The force of the rock on the ground is the product of its mass and the acceleration due to gravity.  The rock has mass kg (about 102 grams); lifting it one metre takes 1 J of work.  The force of the rock is one newton, or one kilogram-metre per second squared, so   Therefore, the mass of the rock is kg, or about 102 grams.  Now, since one joule is one newton-metre, the amount of work required to counteract 1 N of gravitational force for one metre is precisely one joule.  Remark: having an idea of how much work a joule is, and how much force a newton is, is a good tool for checking the reasonableness of your work. For example, after this question, if you calculate that a marble weighs 100 N, you can be pretty sure there's an error in your calculation.  "
