  "title": "Work and Centre of Mass.",
  "body": "Work and Centre of Mass  Here is another example in which an extended body acts like a point particle located at its centre of mass. Imagine that there are a finite number of masses arrayed along a (vertical) -axis with mass number attached at height . Note that the total mass of the array is and that the centre of mass of the array is at height Now suppose that we lift all of the masses, against gravity, to height . So after the lift there is a total mass located at height . The mass is subject to a downward gravitational force of . So to lift the mass we need to apply a compensating upward force of through a distance of . This takes work . So the total work required to lift all masses is      So the work required to lift the array of particles is identical to the work required to lift a single particle, whose mass, , is the total mass of the array, from height , the centre of mass of the array, to height .  "
