  "body": "Centroid of a triangle and its medians  Prove that the centroid of any triangle is located at the point of intersection of the medians. A median of a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.      Solution: Choose a coordinate system so that the vertices of the triangle are located at , and . (In the figure below, is negative.)     The line joining and has equation . (Check that and both really are on this line.) The line joining and has equation . (Check that and both really are on this line.) Hence for each fixed between and , runs from to .  We'll use horizontal strips to compute and . We could just apply equation ( ) with , , (which is gotten by solving for ) and (which is gotten by solving for ).  But rather than memorizing or looking up those formulae, we'll derive them for this example. So consider a thin strip at height as illustrated in the figure above.  The strip has length   The strip has width .  On this strip, has average value .  On this strip, has average value .  As the area of the triangle is , We have found that the centroid of the triangle is at . We shall now show that this point lies on all three medians.  One vertex is at . The opposite side runs from and and so has midpoint . The line from to has slope and so has equation . As , the centroid does indeed lie on this median. In this computation we have implicitly assumed that so that the denominator . In the event that , the median runs from to and so has equation . When we also have , so that the centroid still lies on the median.  Another vertex is at . The opposite side runs from and and so has midpoint . The line from to has slope and so has equation . As , the centroid does indeed lie on this median. In this computation we have implicitly assumed that so that the denominator . In the event that , the median runs from to and so has equation . When we also have , so that the centroid still lies on the median.  The third vertex is at . The opposite side runs from and and so has midpoint . The line from to has slope and so has equation . As , the centroid does indeed lie on this median. This time, we have implicitly assumed that . In the event that , the median runs from to and so has equation . When we also have , so that the centroid still lies on the median.    "
