  "url": "chap_seq_ser-3.html#chap_seq_ser-3-4-2-3",
  "type": "Exercise",
  "number": "3.1.2.10",
  "title": "",
  "body": " Determine the limits of the following sequences.             You can compare the leading terms, or factor a high power of from the numerator and denominator.  (a) (b) (c) 0  When determining the end behaviour of rational functions, recall from last semester that we can either cancel out the highest power of from the numerator and denominator, or skip this step and compare the highest powers of the numerator and denominator.   Since the numerator has a higher degree than the denominator, this sequence will diverge to positive or negative infinity; since its terms are positive for large , its limit is (positive) infinity. (You can imagine that the numerator is growing much, much faster than the denominator, leading the terms to have a very, very large absolute value.)  Calculating the longer way:     Since the numerator has the same degree as the denominator, as goes to infinity, this sequence will converge to the ratio of their leading coefficients: . (You can imagine that the numerator is growing at roughly the same rate as the denominator, so the terms settle into an almost-constant ratio.)  Calculating the longer way:    Since the numerator has a lower degree than the denominator, this sequence will converge to 0 as goes to infinity. (You can imagine that the denominator is growing much, much faster than the numerator, leading the terms to be very, very small.)  Calculating the longer way:      "
