  "body": " The test:  Let . We'll now use the integral test to determine whether or not the series (which is sometimes called the -series) converges.  To do so, we need a function that obeys for all bigger than some . Certainly obeys for all . So let's pick this and try . (We can always increase later if we need to.)   This function also obeys the other two conditions of Theorem :   for all and   decreases as increases because for all .     So the integral test tells us that the series converges if and only if the integral converges.  We have already seen, in Example , that the integral converges if and only if .  So we conclude that converges if and only if . This is sometimes called the -test.  In particular, the series , which is called the harmonic series, has and so diverges. As we add more and more terms of this series together, the terms we add, namely , get smaller and smaller and tend to zero, but they tend to zero so slowly that the full sum is still infinite.  On the other hand, the series has and so converges. This time as we add more and more terms of this series together, the terms we add, namely , tend to zero (just) fast enough that the full sum is finite. Mind you, for this example, the convergence takes place very slowly you have to take a huge number of terms to get a decent approximation to the full sum. If we approximate by the truncated series , we make an error of at most This does tend to zero as , but really slowly.    "
