  "title": "Roots of Polynomials",
  "body": " Roots of Polynomials   Being able to factor polynomials is a very important part of many of the computations in this course. Related to this is the process of finding roots (or zeros) of polynomials. That is, given a polynomial , find all numbers so that .  In the case of a quadratic , we can use the formula The corresponding formulas for cubics and quartics The method for cubics was developed in the 15th century by del Ferro, Cardano and Ferrari (Cardano's student). Ferrari then went on to discover a formula for the roots of a quartic. His formula requires the solution of an associated cubic polynomial. are extremely cumbersome, and no such formula exists for polynomials of degree 5 and higher This is the famous Abel-Ruffini theorem. .  Despite this there are many tricks There is actually a large body of mathematics devoted to developing methods for factoring polynomials. Polynomial factorisation is a fundamental problem for most computer algebra systems. The interested reader should make use of their favourite search engine to find out more. for finding roots of polynomials that work well in some situations but not all. Here we describe approaches that will help you find integer and rational roots of polynomials that will work well on exams, quizzes and homework assignments.  Consider the quadratic equation . We could We probably shouldn't do it this way for such a simple polynomial, but for pedagogical purposes we do here. solve this using the quadratic formula Hence has roots and so it factors as . Notice Many of you may have been taught this approach in highschool. that the numbers and divide the constant term of the polynomial, . This happens in general and forms the basis of our first trick.  A very useful trick  If or is an integer root of a polynomial with integer coefficients, then is a factor of the constant term .    If is a root of the polynomial we know that . Hence If we isolate in this expression we get We can see that divides every term on the right-hand side. This means that the right-hand side is an integer times . Thus the left-hand side, being , is an integer times , as required. The argument for when is a root is almost identical.   Let us put this observation to work.  Integer roots of  Find the integer roots of .  Solution:   The constant term in this polynomial is .  The only divisors of are . So the only candidates for integer roots are .  Trying each in turn   Thus the only integer root is .     Integer roots of   Find the integer roots of .  Solution:   The constant term is .  The divisors of are . So the only candidates for integer roots are .  We try each in turn (it is tedious but not difficult):   Thus the only integer roots are and .     We can generalise this approach in order to find rational roots. Consider the polynomial . We can find its zeros using the quadratic formula: Notice now that the numerators, 1 and 2, both divide the constant term of the polynomial (being 2). Similarly, the denominators, 2 and 3, both divide the coefficient of the highest power of (being 6). This is quite general.  Another nice trick  If or is a rational root in lowest terms (i.e. and are integers with no common factors) of a polynomial with integer coefficients, then the numerator is a factor of the constant term and the denominator is a factor of .    Since is a root of we know that Multiply this equation through by to get Move terms around to isolate : Now every term on the right-hand side is some integer times . Thus the left-hand side must also be an integer times . We know that does not contain any factors of , hence must be some integer times (as required).  Similarly we can isolate the term : Now every term on the right-hand side is some integer times . Thus the left-hand side must also be an integer times . We know that does not contain any factors of , hence must be some integer times (as required).  The argument when is a root is nearly identical.   We should put this to work:  Rational roots of   .  Solution:   The constant term in this polynomial is and the coefficient of the highest power of is .  Thus the only candidates for integer roots are .  By our newest trick, the only candidates for fractional roots are .  We try each in turn Again, this is a little tedious, but not difficult. Its actually pretty easy to code up for a computer to do. Modern polynomial factoring algorithms do more sophisticated things, but these are a pretty good way to start.  so the roots are and .     The tricks above help us to find integer and rational roots of polynomials. With a little extra work we can extend those methods to help us factor polynomials. Say we have a polynomial of degree and have established that is one of its roots. That is, we know . Then we can factor out from  it is always possible to find a polynomial of degree so that   In sufficiently simple cases, you can probably do this factoring by inspection. For example, has as a root because . In this case, so that . As another example, has as a root because . In this case, so that .  For higher degree polynomials we need to use something more systematic long divison.  Long Division  Once you have found a root of a polynomial, even if you cannot factor out of the polynomial by inspection, you can find by dividing by , using the long division algorithm you learned This is a standard part of most highschool mathematics curricula, but perhaps not all. You should revise this carefully. in school, but with replaced by .   Roots of  Factor .  Solution:   We can go hunting for integer roots of the polynomial by looking at the divisors of the constant term. This tells us to try .  A quick computation shows that while . Hence is a root of the polynomial and so must be a factor.   So we divide . The first term, , in the quotient is chosen so that when you multiply it by the denominator, , the leading term, , matches the leading term in the numerator, , exactly.       When you subtract from the numerator you get the remainder . Just like in public school, the is not normally brought down until it is actually needed.       The next term, , in the quotient is chosen so that when you multiply it by the denominator, , the leading term matches the leading term in the remainder exactly.     And so on.       Note that we finally end up with a remainder . A nonzero remainder would have signalled a computational error, since we know that the denominator must divide the numerator exactly.   We conclude that To check this, just multiply out the left hand side explicitly.  Applying the high school quadratic root formula to tells us that it has no real roots and that we cannot factor it further Because we are not permitted to use complex numbers. .     We finish by describing an alternative to long division. The approach is roughly equivalent, but is perhaps more straightforward at the expense of requiring more algebra.  Roots of again  Factor , again.   Solution: Let us do this again but avoid long division.  From the previous example, we know that must be a polynomial (since is a root of the numerator) of degree 2. So write for some, as yet unknown, coefficients and .  Cross multiplying and simplifying gives us   Now matching coefficients of the various powers of on the left and right hand sides   This gives us a system of equations that we can solve quite directly. Indeed it tells us immediately that that and . Subbing into tells us that and hence .  Thus       "
