  "number": "B.1",
  "title": "Definition and Basic Operations",
  "body": " Definition and Basic Operations  We'll start with the definition of a complex number and its addition and multiplication rules. You may find the multiplication rule quite mysterious. Don't worry. We'll soon gets lots of practice using it and seeing how useful it is.     The complex plane is simply the -plane equipped with an addition operation and a multiplication operation. A complex number is nothing more than a point in that -plane. It is conventional to use the notation In electrical engineering it is coonventional to use instead of . to stand for the complex number . In other words, it is conventional to write in place of and in place of . The first component, , of the complex number is called its real part and the second component, , is called its imaginary part, even though there is nothing imaginary Don't attempt to attribute any special significance to the word complex in complex number , or to the word real in real number and real part , or to the word imaginary in imaginary part . All are just names . The name imaginary was introduced by René Descartes in 1637. René Descartes (1596 1650) was a French scientist and philosopher, who lived in the Dutch Republic for roughly twenty years after serving in the (mercenary) Dutch States Army. Originally, imaginary was a derogatory term and imaginary numbers were thought to be useless. But they turned out to be incredibly useful! about it. The sum of the complex numbers and is defined by That is, you just separately add the real parts and the imaginary parts. The product of the complex numbers and is defined by Do not memorise this multiplication rule. We'll see a simple, effective memory aid for it shortly. The heart of that memory aid is the observation that the complex number has the special property that      Addition and multiplication of complex numbers obey the familiar addition rules and multiplication rules and distributive laws To remember how to multiply complex numbers, you just have to supplement the familiar rules of the real number system with . The previous sentence is the memory aid referred to in Definition (d).   If and , then      The negative of any complex number is defined by and obviously obeys . The reciprocal The reciprocal is also called the multiplicative inverse of . , or , of any complex number , other than , is defined by We shall see below that it is given by the formula      It is possible to derive the formula for by observing that equals if and only if and solving these equations for and . We will see a much shorter derivation in Remark below. For now, we'll content ourselves with just verifying that is the inverse of by multiplying out      The complex conjugate of is denoted and is defined to be That is, to take the complex conjugate, one replaces every by and vice versa. The distance from (recall that this is the point in the -plane) to is denoted and is called the absolute value, or modulus, of . It is given by Note that is always a nonnegative real number and that      Let with and real. Since , we have which is the formula for given in Definition (b).    It is easy to divide a complex number by a real number. For example In general, the complex conjugate provides us with a trick for rewriting any ratio of complex numbers as a ratio with a real denominator. For example, suppose that we want to find . The trick is to multiply by . The number is the complex conjugate of the denominator . Since      The notations The symbols and are the letters and in the Fraktur font, which was created in the early 1500's and became common in the German-speaking world. A standard alternative notation is and .  and stand for the real and imaginary parts of the complex number , respectively. If (with and real) they are defined by Note that both and are real numbers. Just subbing in , you can verify that     If and , then     Since ,    "
